WEBVTT

00:00:00.395 --> 00:00:02.920
Listener: Why is the refund for order one
oh four stuck?

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.865
Guide: The refund job called the payment
API.

00:00:05.865 --> 00:00:08.040
It waited for a reply and timed out.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:08.990
See those dots?

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:10.790
Each is a record from one system.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.515
Kubernetes lists workloads.

00:00:12.515 --> 00:00:14.665
A network flow names two endpoints.

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:16.190
A trace records a call.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.715
The refund job’s call is our starting
point.

00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:22.262
Listener: What ties this reading to that
storage account?

00:00:22.807 --> 00:00:25.257
Guide: They share a full Azure resource
ID.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:27.632
Azure Resource Graph lists the storage
account,

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:30.282
and Azure Monitor puts that ID on its
reading.

00:00:30.282 --> 00:00:31.982
Now follow the DNS name.

00:00:31.982 --> 00:00:33.657
It resolves to an address.

00:00:33.657 --> 00:00:36.807
The private endpoint at that address names
the storage account.

00:00:36.807 --> 00:00:39.207
That gives us a path from the name to
storage.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:42.687
Listener: What happens to that path when
we group the dots?

00:00:43.232 --> 00:00:46.832
Guide: Watch the DNS name, the private
endpoint and the storage account.

00:00:46.832 --> 00:00:49.382
They move into different groups and stay
connected.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.132
This projection groups records by
technology.

00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:54.432
Kubernetes workloads cluster together.

00:00:54.432 --> 00:00:57.157
Azure resources, network connections, DNS,

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:00.307
application telemetry and shop software
each get a group.

00:01:01.537 --> 00:01:03.412
Listener: Where does that leave the refund
job?

00:01:03.957 --> 00:01:05.432
Guide: It belongs to Payout.

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:09.932
The job reads the case store in Returns
Intake and calls the payment API.

00:01:09.932 --> 00:01:12.457
Now we’re grouping software by the job it
does.

00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:15.432
Those links show what Payout needs from
the other groups.

00:01:15.432 --> 00:01:17.332
Shared infrastructure has a group too,

00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:19.607
so we can still trace the path to storage.

00:01:20.828 --> 00:01:23.378
Listener: Which view would help me
investigate the timeout?

00:01:23.898 --> 00:01:25.273
Guide: Start with the dashboard.

00:01:25.273 --> 00:01:27.748
Successful payment calls drop to zero.

00:01:28.228 --> 00:01:31.478
Open the health tree and you can see why
that blocks Payout.

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:33.408
Want to find the pods?

00:01:33.408 --> 00:01:37.408
The Kubernetes view starts at the refund
deployment and follows it to them.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:40.688
The Azure resource view lists the storage
account.

00:01:41.193 --> 00:01:45.368
In the DNS and IP view, its name resolves
to a private address.

00:01:45.368 --> 00:01:47.943
All five views draw on the same shop
records.

00:01:49.603 --> 00:01:53.253
Listener: If the database stops answering,
why does web turn red too?

00:01:53.823 --> 00:01:57.498
Guide: Web needs the API, and the API
needs the database.

00:01:57.498 --> 00:02:00.048
Watch the recorded pg-shop outage.

00:02:00.048 --> 00:02:02.248
First the database signal turns red.

00:02:02.248 --> 00:02:04.723
Then you see that failure on the API and
web.

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:06.023
That’s a health roll-up.

00:02:06.023 --> 00:02:09.298
A caller’s state reflects what happened to
a service it depends on.

00:02:10.495 --> 00:02:12.845
Listener: What makes the database red in
the first place?

00:02:13.415 --> 00:02:16.465
Guide: At ten eleven, its availability
meter reads zero.

00:02:16.465 --> 00:02:19.115
Azure Monitor reports the sample a minute
later.

00:02:19.115 --> 00:02:21.365
That’s enough for the shop’s database
rule.

00:02:21.365 --> 00:02:23.390
One bad minute opens the alarm.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.415
The reading tells us what the meter saw.

00:02:25.415 --> 00:02:28.190
The rule decides when that reading means
Unhealthy.

00:02:29.437 --> 00:02:32.237
Listener: Why does the alarm reach web and
leave the cache green?

00:02:32.782 --> 00:02:35.432
Guide: Start at web and follow the arrows
marked Affects.

00:02:35.432 --> 00:02:37.932
There’s a path through the API to the
database.

00:02:37.932 --> 00:02:40.082
The structure query builds those links.

00:02:40.082 --> 00:02:42.257
The health query follows up to sixteen of
them

00:02:42.257 --> 00:02:44.582
and keeps the worst severity it finds.

00:02:44.582 --> 00:02:48.007
Start at the cache, though, and no path
leads to the failing server.

00:02:48.007 --> 00:02:49.532
The cache stays green.

00:02:50.745 --> 00:02:52.095
Listener: The meter is back at one.

00:02:52.095 --> 00:02:54.020
Why is the database still red?

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.640
Guide: That’s one good minute.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.465
The rule needs two in a row.

00:02:57.465 --> 00:02:59.240
Ten fourteen is the first.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.390
At ten fifteen, the alarm clears and the
callers recover.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.640
You can see the failure in graph revision
sixteen

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.265
and the recovery in eighteen.

00:03:06.265 --> 00:03:08.265
The revision numbers count changes.

00:03:08.265 --> 00:03:10.015
The meter gives us the times.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.020
Listener: Suppose an API pod starts
sending traffic to Redis.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.645
What does Sattle see first?

00:03:17.215 --> 00:03:18.740
Guide: One new Hubble flow.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.915
In this copy of the pg-shop recording,

00:03:20.915 --> 00:03:24.315
an API pod sends traffic to an existing
Redis cache.

00:03:24.315 --> 00:03:27.215
The pod and the destination are already on
the map.

00:03:27.215 --> 00:03:31.015
The new line on the master graph connects
the pod to that destination.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:35.845
Listener: How does that address become a
Redis dependency in the health model?

00:03:36.365 --> 00:03:39.340
Guide: Kubernetes tells us which service
the pod belongs to.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.640
It’s the API.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.890
At the other end, the address matches a
private endpoint for Redis,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.515
and the host name identifies the same
cache.

00:03:46.515 --> 00:03:49.765
The existing structure query links the API
to Redis.

00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:53.540
Now when the cache is Degraded, so are the
API and its gateway.

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.145
Listener: Redis was already there.

00:03:56.145 --> 00:03:57.720
Where can I find the new link?

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.490
Guide: Compare the two revisions in the
Canvas.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:03.915
The patch lists the link it added, from
the API to Redis.

00:04:03.915 --> 00:04:05.690
That’s the change you see highlighted.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:08.440
The existing health rule follows that
dependency.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.240
A warning on the cache can now reach the
API.

00:04:12.478 --> 00:04:16.078
Listener: How could I check that the API
really started using that cache?

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:18.373
Guide: Open the evidence for the link.

00:04:18.373 --> 00:04:20.498
The structure query kept two matches.

00:04:20.498 --> 00:04:24.098
One names the pod, the destination address
and the private endpoint.

00:04:24.098 --> 00:04:25.848
The other names the Redis host.

00:04:25.848 --> 00:04:28.398
Both lead back to the flow and Azure
records.

00:04:28.398 --> 00:04:30.673
The traffic explains the new dependency.

00:04:30.673 --> 00:04:33.298
The cache’s own load readings explain the
warning.
